+# Architecture: Takoboto-Class Kanji Detail UX & Visual Engine (Phase 14.4F)
+
+## 1. Overview
+
+The Kanji Detail UX (`/kanji/[character]`) and its supporting backend endpoints provide comprehensive character intelligence modeled after Takoboto. It unites canonical lexical data from KANJIDIC2 (13,108 kanji), vector graphics from KanjiVG (11,658 SVGs), and the bidirectional lexical graph connecting kanji to 206,747 dictionary entries.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Page Architecture & Component Layout
+
+```
+┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
+│                       Kanji Hero Header                                 │
+│  [ 大 ]  Meaning: "big, large"  |  Grade: 1  |  JLPT: N5  |  Strokes: 3  │
+├───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┤
+│      Stroke Order Visualizer      │           Radical & Components      │
+│  - Static Numbered Diagram        │  - Radical: 大 (No. 37, 3 strokes)   │
+│  - Animated Vector SVG            │  - Constituent parts: 一, 人        │
+│  - Speed Controls & Play/Pause    │  - Semantic classification          │
+├───────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┤
+│                      Reading Taxonomy & Alignment                       │
+│  - On'yomi: ダイ, タイ             - Kun'yomi: おお-, おお.きい          │
+│  - Special/Irregular: 大人 (おとな - Jukujikun), 大和 (やまと - Ateji)   │
+├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
+│                   Lexical Graph Vocabulary Explorer                     │
+│  Filter by Reading: [All] [ダイ] [タイ] [おお.きい]                      │
+│  Filter by JLPT:    [All] [N5] [N4] [N3] [N2] [N1]                      │
+│  Filter by Position:[All] [Prefix (大*)] [Middle (*大*)] [Suffix (*大)] │
+│                                                                         │
+│  Result Cards: 大学 (だいがく), 大人 (おとな), 大きい (おおきい), etc.    │
+├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
+│                       Kanji Mind Tree / Graph                           │
+│  Radial visual hierarchy, JLPT branching, and structural derivation     │
+└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
+```
+
+---
+
+## 3. KanjiVG Stroke Order Visualizer
+
+The visual engine is powered by `KanjiVisualService`, operating over 11,658 KanjiVG vector definitions stored locally in `data/kanjivg/`:
+
+1. **Static Diagram**:
+   - Parses the XML SVG structure.
+   - Extracts stroke paths (`kvg:type`) and stroke number coordinates (`kvg:element`).
+   - Renders numbered start-point indicators for each stroke with distinct high-contrast colors (e.g. crimson `#dc2626` text on subtle background).
+2. **Animated Stroke Order**:
+   - Generates pure CSS keyframe animations for each stroke path.
+   - Utilizes `stroke-dasharray` and `stroke-dashoffset` techniques to animate drawing sequentially from stroke 1 to N.
+   - Supports looping or single-pass playback.
+3. **Prefers-Reduced-Motion Compliance**:
+   - Employs `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` in CSS.
+   - Defaults to static stroke display when reduced motion is detected on client devices, providing manual play overrides for accessibility.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Stroke Count Discrepancy Handling: The `箸` Invariant
+
+A central invariant of Phase 14.4 is preserving canonical data integrity while accurately rendering visual assets:
+- **Canonical Truth**: KANJIDIC2 records `箸` (chopsticks) as **14 strokes** (`kanji_entries.stroke_count = 14`), confirmed by the Phase 14.4C conflict decision (`KEEP_EXISTING`).
+- **Visual Reality**: The KanjiVG stroke vector file for `07bb8.svg` contains **15 distinct stroke paths** (`d="..."`).
+- **Resolution**:
+  - The database row is **never modified** or bumped to 15.
+  - The API returns both metrics: `strokeCount: 14` and `visualStrokeCount: 15`.
+  - An explicit discrepancy flag `hasDiscrepancy: true` and explanation note are generated:
+    ```json
+    {
+      "strokeCount": 14,
+      "visualStrokeCount": 15,
+      "hasDiscrepancy": true,
+      "discrepancyNote": "Canonical stroke count is 14 (KANJIDIC2), visual diagram has 15 strokes"
+    }
+    ```
+  - The web UI renders a non-destructive informational badge alerting the student to the 14 vs 15 discrepancy without altering canonical metrics.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Radical & Structural Decomposition
+
+The kanji is analyzed through two structural dimensions:
+1. **Kangxi Radical**: Extracted from `kanji_radicals` (63 core radicals indexed) or KANJIDIC2 radical references, surfacing radical name, radical number, stroke count, and meaning.
+2. **Constituent Components**: Decomposed into sub-parts (e.g. `明` -> `日` [sun] + `月` [moon]) via `kanji_composition`, allowing users to drill into parent and child kanji recursively.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Lexical Graph Vocabulary Exploration
+
+Vocabulary containing the target kanji are retrieved via `KanjiLexicalGraphService` and exposed via `/api/kanji/[character]/vocabulary`:
+- **Reading Grouping**: Words are aligned to specific kanji readings (e.g. grouping words using `ダイ` vs words using `タイ`).
+- **Special Readings**: Identification of non-standard readings, including Jukujikun (e.g. `今日` -> `きょう`) and Ateji (e.g. `寿司` -> `すし`).
+- **Positional Classification**:
+  - `prefix`: Kanji occupies the initial position (index 0).
+  - `middle`: Kanji occupies a non-boundary interior position (index > 0 and < length - 1).
+  - `suffix`: Kanji occupies the final position (index length - 1).
+- **JLPT Filtering**: Scoped retrieval by JLPT proficiency level (`N5` through `N1`).
