+# Tatoeba Canonical Linkage Model & Topology (Phase 14.5B)
+
+## 1. Relational Topology Overview
+
+NihongoBridge links normalized example sentences to canonical entities through deterministic, read-only graph edges:
+
+```
+                          ┌────────────────────────┐
+                          │   Tatoeba Sentence     │
+                          │   (es-tat-1001)        │
+                          └───────────┬────────────┘
+                                      │
+            ┌─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┐
+            │                         │                        │
+            ▼                         ▼                        ▼
+┌───────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐
+│     Kanji Linkage     │ │  Dictionary Linkage   │ │  Translation Linkage  │
+│ - character: 冷       │ │ - headword: 水        │ │ - targetId: 2001      │
+│ - kanjiId: ke-冷      │ │ - entryId: de-mizu    │ │ - lang: eng           │
+│ - position: 0         │ │ - match: EXACT        │ │ - rel: DIRECT_TRANS   │
+│ - occurrenceIndex: 0  │ │ - position: 3         │ │ - text: I drank...    │
+│ - isCanonical: true   │ │ - jlpt: N5            │ └───────────────────────┘
+└───────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘
+            │                         │
+            └────────────┬────────────┘
+                         ▼
+             ┌───────────────────────┐
+             │  Derived JLPT Profile │
+             │ - highest: N5         │
+             │ - lowest: N5          │
+             │ - linkedN5Count: 2    │
+             └───────────────────────┘
+```
+
+---
+
+## 2. Kanji Linkage & Positional Indexing
+
+### 2.1 Derivation Rules
+1. **Literal Occurrence**: Scans the sentence string across Unicode code points.
+2. **Positional Index**: Records the 0-based index of each kanji character in the sentence string.
+3. **Occurrence Index**: Records repeated occurrences of the same kanji character (e.g. `春` at index 0 is occurrence 0, `春` at index 5 is occurrence 1).
+4. **Canonical Assertion**: Checks character presence against `kanji_entries` (13,108 canonical kanji).
+   - Known kanji: `isCanonical = true`, `kanjiId = ke-<char>`.
+   - Unknown kanji: `isCanonical = false`, `kanjiId = null`, triggers `UNKNOWN_KANJI_CHARACTER` warning.
+5. **Canonical Stroke Invariant**: Canonical stroke counts (such as `箸` with 14 strokes) remain strictly untouched in canonical storage.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Canonical Dictionary Linkage
+
+### 3.1 Headword Alignment
+- Matches sentence substrings against canonical `dictionary_entries.headword`.
+- **False Positive Protection**: Matches are scoped to headwords with $\ge 2$ characters or headwords containing kanji, preventing accidental single-kana particle collisions (e.g. matching grammatical `は` or `が` against rare 1-kana dictionary entries).
+- **Match Types**:
+  - `EXACT_HEADWORD`: Literal appearance of the dictionary headword in the sentence.
+  - `READING_MATCH`: Phonetic match against dictionary reading.
+  - `KANJI_CONTAINMENT_ONLY`: Containment of kanji without full word alignment.
+  - `UNRESOLVED`: Fallback when no deterministic word match is confirmed.
+
+---
+
+## 4. JLPT Profile Derivation
+
+NihongoBridge **never fabricates or guesses** a whole-sentence JLPT grade. Instead, JLPT characteristics are derived deterministically from linked canonical vocabulary and kanji:
+
+- **`sentenceJLPTHighest`**: The most advanced JLPT level present among linked entities ($N1 > N2 > N3 > N4 > N5$).
+- **`sentenceJLPTLowest`**: The simplest JLPT level present among linked entities.
+- **Level Counts**: Discrete counts of entities linked to N5, N4, N3, N2, N1, and unclassified.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Translation Relationship Topology
+
+The linkage engine supports diverse translation topologies without flattening:
+1. **One-to-One (1:1)**: Single Japanese sentence linked to a single foreign translation.
+2. **One-to-Many (1:N)**: Single Japanese sentence linked to multiple distinct translations (e.g. literal vs conversational nuance).
+3. **Many-to-One (N:1)**: Multiple distinct Japanese expressions (e.g. formal vs informal) linked to the same foreign translation node.
+4. **Untranslated (0:N)**: Authentic Japanese sentences lacking foreign translations, preserved with `UNTRANSLATED` status.
